# Living Survey: Quantum_Computing                                                                             
                                                                                                               
                                                                                                               
                                                                                                               
## Introduction                                                                                                
                                                                                                               
                                                                                                               
                                                                                                               
This document collects the scientific literature regarding **Quantum_Computing**.                              
                                                                                                               

I will create sections based on themes:                                                                        

 • Quantum Algorithms & Optimization                                                                           
 • Quantum Simulation & Materials Science                                                                      
 • Quantum Cryptography & Communication                                                                        
 • Quantum Computing Applications (CFD, etc.)                                                                  
 • Post-Quantum Cryptography & Security                                                                        
 • Future Architectures & Paradigms                                                                            

Let's draft the markdown content carefully, ensuring no LaTeX, pure markdown, and citations like [^id].        

Quantum Algorithms & Optimization                                                                              

Recent research continues to explore the intersection of quantum mechanics and optimization algorithms.        
Granular computing has been proposed to hierarchically reduce search spaces, allowing quantum genetic          
optimization to identify near-optimal solutions even on limited-qubit processors                               
[[^10.1007_s41066-022-00348-9]]. Furthermore, foundational reviews emphasize the counterintuitive nature of    
quantum bits (qubits) and their potential to disrupt conventional digital logic through superposition and      
entanglement [[^10.5120_ijca2021921834]].                                                                      

Quantum Simulation & Materials Science                                                                         

The application of quantum computers to computational chemistry and materials science is advancing rapidly. A  
practical guide demonstrates how quantum annealers can calculate the relative energy of defective graphene     
structures, translating chemical models into qubit representations [[^10.1063_5.0151346]]. Additionally,       
specialized software like PULSEE has been developed to simulate nuclear spin dynamics and magnetic resonance   
observables, bridging quantum information science with condensed matter research                               
[[^10.1016_j.cpc.2022.108598]].                                                                                

Quantum Cryptography & Communication                                                                           

Secure communication remains a primary driver for quantum research. A new approach to quantum cryptography     
outlines protocols such as BB84, B92, and B91, alongside analyses of Shor's algorithm and various attack       
vectors like photon splitting and faked-state attacks [[^https:__openalex.org_W7112574406]]. Practical         
implementations are also being documented, including detailed Python simulations of the BB84 protocol that     
model state preparation, eavesdropping, and privacy amplification [[^10.6084_m9.figshare.32051724]]. The       
broader vision of a Quantum Internet is also gaining traction, with studies providing primers on how quantum   
networks will transform global connectivity and security [[^10.31695_ijerat.2025.9.1]].                        

Quantum Computing Applications in Engineering                                                                  

Beyond theory, quantum computing is being applied to complex engineering problems. Computational Fluid Dynamics
(CFD) simulations for aeronautical heat exchangers are being explored through quantum algorithms, including    
approaches based on the Lattice Boltzmann Method and quantum machine learning                                  
[[^10.11159_icffts23.133]][^10.21741_9781644902813-38]]. While still in early development stages, these        
applications highlight the potential for quantum architectures to solve high-dimensional fluid dynamics        
problems.                                                                                                      

Post-Quantum Cryptography & Security                                                                           

As quantum capabilities grow, the security of classical cryptographic systems is under scrutiny. Research into 
the Learning With Errors (LWE) problem has led to improved dual lattice attacks that challenge the security    
assumptions of post-quantum finalists like Kyber, Saber, and Dilithium [[^10.5281_zenodo.6493704]].            
Concurrently, hybrid encryption systems are being designed to withstand quantum threats, incorporating dynamic 
compression and dual authentication mechanisms alongside post-quantum cryptographic strategies                 
[[^10.56975_tijer.v13i3.161368]].                                                                              

Future Architectures & Paradigms                                                                               

The landscape of future computing extends beyond traditional digital and quantum models. Analyses of           
open-source repositories and financial news indicate emerging trends in quantum software ecosystems            
[[^10.1007_978-3-031-90727-2_20]]. Alternative paradigms, such as atom computing, are proposed to overcome the 
material scaling constraints of transistor-based systems and the conceptual limitations of current qubit       
architectures, utilizing controlled electron or photon flows for deterministic switching                       
[[^10.34257_gjsfra257362]]. Comparative studies continue to provide layperson-friendly architectural           
descriptions of digital, quantum, and biological computing systems to standardize industry models              
[[^10.1109_mc.2021.3083430]].                                                                                  

